"""
Lightweight i18n layer for saas_app.py's user-facing text.

WHY A PLAIN PYTHON DICT, NOT JSON FILES
-----------------------------------------
Every other per-user preference in this SaaS layer (phone, country,
broker credentials) is read straight out of saas_platform.db with no
extra file I/O, and the deploy pattern for this whole project is a
single-file scp + py_compile + restart (see engines/tenant_engine.py's
module docstring for the DB side of this). Loading translations from
JSON files would add a second thing that has to be copied to the
droplet in the right relative path and would be resolved relative to
whatever the process's cwd happens to be -- fragile across local dev
vs. the systemd unit's WorkingDirectory. Keeping translations as a
plain dict literal in this module means "deploy the new translations"
is exactly the same one-file scp as every other engine change.

WHY t() TAKES lang EXPLICITLY INSTEAD OF READING st.session_state
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Every other engine in engines/ is plain Python with no Streamlit
dependency, importable by both saas_app.py (interactive) and
saas_decision_engine.py's background scheduler tick (no Streamlit
session at all). This module follows the same rule so it stays usable
from either context in the future, even though today only saas_app.py
calls it. saas_app.py is responsible for reading the current user's
language out of st.session_state (or the DB) and passing it in --
see saas_app.py's _t() wrapper.

SCOPE OF THIS PASS (2026-09-07)
---------------------------------
Covers every static/templated UI string in saas_app.py's own render_*
functions used by end users: auth, broker connections, MT4/5 bridge,
pause control, trading settings, account settings, the AI Trading run
screen (including its results-table column headers), My Positions,
Performance, billing, the main dashboard shell, password reset, email
verification/change, and the legal-page chrome (back buttons + title).
render_admin_panel is deliberately NOT covered -- it's operator-only
tooling seen by nobody but the site owner, not a paying customer in
any of these languages.

UPDATE (2026-09-15): the Terms of Service / Privacy Policy body text
("legal.terms_body" / "legal.privacy_body") and the COUNTRY_OPTIONS
display labels ("country.<English name>") are now translated into all
12 languages too, closing the two gaps called out above in the
original 2026-09-07 pass. Each translated legal document carries a
"legal.authoritative_notice" string (empty for English) noting that
the English version governs in case of translation conflict/ambiguity
-- this is an appropriate machine-translation disclaimer, not a
substitute for professional legal review of the underlying English
text (see saas_app.py's _LEGAL_LAST_UPDATED comment block).
COUNTRY_OPTIONS itself stays a fixed list of canonical English strings
(existing users' stored `country` DB values are English, and
saas_app.py's "Prefer not to say" sentinel comparisons key off the
English literal) -- only the label shown in the selectbox is
translated, via saas_app.py's _country_label() format_func.

Platform dropdown option values ("MT4"/"MT5") remain untranslated --
those are product/brand names, not natural-language content.
"""

